{
  "id": "COMPOUND:18621 - PROTEIN:1146",
  "verbalization": "Colutequinone A appears to interact with Dr hemagglutinin structural subunit through a series of molecular pathways, with the most direct link emerging through its interaction with Forkhead box protein O1. This protein is a target of Velatumolide, which in turn targets Dr hemagglutinin structural subunit. The evidence suggests a chain of targets, where Colutequinone A acts as a modulator of the signaling pathway involving Forkhead box protein O1, which ultimately influences the structural subunit of Dr hemagglutinin. \n\nThe presence of multiple targets, including Replicase polyprotein 1a and Aquilarabietic Acid H, further supports the notion that Colutequinone A may influence a broader biological process that leads to the structural subunit of Dr hemagglutinin. The overlapping targets and the hierarchical nature of the interactions suggest a potential mechanism where Colutequinone A modulates the activity of key proteins in a pathway that ultimately affects the structural subunit of Dr hemagglutinin. \n\nThe integration of these pathways highlights the complexity of the relationship between Colutequinone A and Dr hemagglutinin structural subunit, with the central role of Forkhead box protein O1 and its downstream targets. The evidence supports a mechanism where Colutequinone A may act as a regulator of a signaling cascade that ultimately impacts the structural subunit of Dr hemagglutinin, though the exact nature of this regulatory relationship remains to be fully elucidated.",
